{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790716913,"nonce":"yWKhSglmmWPoCDmbXbomwQ4b2bY=","title":"Support mixed-float native calls beyond eight arguments","message":"The interpreter accepts integer-only native calls with up to twelve argument words, but ash_native_call generates typed mixed integer/f32/f64 dispatch only through eight. A native whose signature has nine or more arguments and at least one floating-point argument currently reaches dispatch_float_native and fails with \"Native dispatch: no signature\" even when the call is valid for the C ABI. The HashLink runtime's method-call path also reserves space for nine arguments.\n\nThe all-signatures-through-eight generator fixes the observed ChimpMonk failures but does not cover this arity gap. Avoid extending the 3^N Rust table to twelve arguments; it would require more than two million call signatures. Add a portable, bounded fallback for larger signatures, or a runtime-generated typed call stub with clear ABI and platform coverage. Include a nine-argument mixed f32/f64 reproducer and verify interpreter/hybrid calls and return values on supported targets. Keep any architecture-specific shortcut out of the generic path unless its calling convention is proved and tested.","files":null}]}